#!/usr/bin/env bash
#
# identity-strings - Agora - unit 001, task T-322.
#
# What it enforces: D-021, "a string whose job is to name the product to
# someone who has no other context carries the full name `Ágora Transparency`;
# a string that refers back to a product already named on the same surface may
# use `Ágora`."
#
# Why a guard and not a deny term (D-021, and this is the load-bearing part):
# `Ágora` is a LEGITIMATE substring of the correct value and of ~33 prose
# occurrences, so a deny term would report ~35 findings on a clean tree and the
# only route to green would be deleting the product's name from its own
# documentation. Decisively: a deny term is an EXPECT-ZERO assertion, and I-028
# says to prefer an expected value that a failure cannot counterfeit. "The
# identity strings are correct" is naturally EXPECT-PRESENT, whose degenerate
# value is `no` and never `yes`.
#
# SIX assertions:
#   1. recipe.yml has exactly ONE `^name:` line, and its value is exactly
#      $FULL_NAME. In the installer's template selector that string IS the
#      entire user interface for "what is this"; a bare `Ágora` sends the
#      reader to a dormant 2019 project of that name.
#   2. composer.json `.name` == $PACKAGE.
#   3. the two `description` fields - recipe.yml and composer.json - are
#      BYTE-IDENTICAL to each other. D-021 has them drop the name rather than
#      expand it, and keeping them in step is the only thing stopping the two
#      surfaces from describing two different products.
#   4. for each identity file, the FIRST mention of `Ágora` names it in full.
#      Expressed as ONE rule rather than as five line numbers, which rot the
#      first time a paragraph is rewrapped.
#   5. CLOSED WORLD: every packaged file that names the product must be
#      declared, either as an identity file or as prose-only. An UNDECLARED
#      new packaged file naming the product is a FINDING. This is the half
#      that matters most: I-024 - "inherited boilerplate outlives the task
#      that was supposed to clean it up whenever no task owns the WHOLE file"
#      - made impossible rather than remembered.
#   6. recipe.yml still has a `recipes:` key with a NON-EMPTY list beneath it,
#      and still carries its `-- area:` labels. Added later than the other
#      five, as a DEFECT FIX rather than as a new invariant; the reasoning is
#      below, because it is the part a future reader will want and the part a
#      future reader will otherwise re-litigate.
#
# WHY ASSERTION 6 LIVES HERE, AND WHY IT IS A DEFECT FIX. D-032 names three
# ways a config export can ruin this repository with NO error anywhere:
# recipe.yml silently replaced, content silently exported into content/, and
# config/ silently empty. The third is closed by tests/bin/config-inventory and
# the second is closed by a content-count assertion. The first was open, and it
# was open in a specific way: `drush site:export` REGENERATES recipe.yml from a
# handful of top-level keys, so the regenerated file has no `recipes:` key at
# all and none of the area comments - while still carrying a correct `name:`
# and a correct `description:`, which is exactly and only what assertions 1 and
# 3 read. A regenerated recipe.yml PASSED this script. That is a hole in an
# existing invariant, not a missing invariant, so it is repaired in the file
# that already opens recipe.yml, already declares its scope, already prints its
# counts and already runs inside the blocking agora-invariants job. A separate
# script would have been a second thing to remember to run.
#
# WHY IT ASSERTS STRUCTURE AND NOT A CENSUS. The list holds 10 entries today
# and units 004 and 005 add more, so `entries == 10` would have to be relaxed
# every time the product grows - and a gate that is routinely relaxed is the
# gate that teaches people to relax gates. T-601's row made that ruling and it
# applies unchanged here. So the ASSERTION is structural (the key exists, the
# list is not empty) and the CENSUS is printed beside it: the number is
# evidence for a reader, never a threshold for the script.
#
# The `-- area:` floor is 15 for the same reason and with the same honesty
# about what it discriminates: there are 16 matching lines today - the 15 area
# labels of D-011 rider b, plus the one line of prose that DEFINES the
# convention - and the failure this catches is the wholesale loss of every
# comment in the file, which takes the count to 0, not the loss of one label.
# Stated rather than implied, per I-045: a floor of 15 is a claim about
# regeneration, not a claim that all 15 labels are individually present.
#
# WHY IT IS GUARDED ON THE FILE EXISTING. This script is shared with
# drupal/agora_theme as a hash-pinned ADAPTED copy (D-028), and a theme has no
# recipe.yml; an unconditional read would turn the theme's gate red the moment
# its copy is refreshed. The guard costs this repository nothing, because here
# recipe.yml is a DECLARED IDENTITY FILE and the preflight above already exits
# FATAL when a declared identity file is unreadable - the skip branch is
# unreachable in this repository by construction, which is the only kind of
# conditional that is not a hole.
#
# WHY ASSERTION 4 NORMALISES BEFORE MATCHING. A naive "the first line
# containing `Ágora` also contains `Ágora Transparency`" is wrong on this tree
# TODAY, and not because the tree is wrong: AGENTS.md's first mention is a
# Markdown blockquote whose full name WRAPS across the newline, so `Ágora` ends
# line 5 and `Transparency` begins line 6 behind a `> ` marker. A line-oriented
# check would report a finding against correct prose, and the tempting repair -
# a hand-placed line break - is the rot the rule exists to avoid. So each file
# is read as ONE stream with line-leading `>` and `#` markers dropped and all
# runs of whitespace collapsed to a single space; the assertion is then that
# the text FOLLOWING the first `Ágora` begins with ` Transparency`.
#
# An identity file containing NO accented mention at all is NOT a finding -
# composer.json is exactly that case, and it is covered by assertion 2 instead.
# But if NO identity file has one, the accent match itself has broken and every
# assertion 4 has silently stopped discriminating: that is FATAL (I-028).
#
# SCOPE, in two parts, mirroring no-code-in-template:
#   (1) AUTHORITATIVE: the packaged tree, literally `git archive HEAD`, which
#       is what Drupal.org ships.
#   (2) EARLY WARNING: untracked, non-ignored working-tree files that are not
#       under an `export-ignore`d root, so a new undeclared file naming the
#       product is caught BEFORE it is committed rather than after (I-030).
# Part 2 reads the RULE in .gitattributes rather than its effect, which is
# weaker than part 1 on purpose and is never the only witness; part 1 reads the
# effect. CONTENT is read from the working tree wherever the path exists there,
# so an uncommitted edit is seen (I-018).
#
# Contract (shared by every invariant in tests/bin/):
#   scope:    the exact set of files inspected
#   scanned:  N files, and N must be > 0 or this script fails
#   findings: M, each printed as  path:line  reason
#   exit 0 if and only if M == 0
#
# FATAL, not an ordinary finding, and printing NO summary line (I-029): a
# declared identity file missing from the working tree; `git archive HEAD`
# producing nothing; zero identity files declared; zero packaged files naming
# the product; zero identity files carrying an accented mention; any `grep`
# exiting >= 2. The closing metrics line is a claim about a scan that happened.
#
# House rules for tests/bin/ (T-321(c)): `wc -l` never `grep -c` for a compared
# value; numeric guards defaulted ONLY where zero means FAIL; grep rc >= 2 is an
# error, never "no match"; never `-F` with `-i`. The CANONICAL copy of the block,
# with the reasoning and the I-031 asymmetry spelled out, lives in the header of
# tests/bin/no-ci-allow-dev. This script was born under those rules rather than
# swept into them: there is no `grep -c` anywhere below.
#
# Usage: tests/bin/identity-strings   (run from anywhere; it cd's to the repo root)

set -u
# No `set -e`: a finding must be recorded and counted, not abort the run.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- repo root --
SCRIPT_DIR=$(CDPATH= cd -- "$(dirname -- "$0")" && pwd)
REPO_ROOT=$(CDPATH= cd -- "$SCRIPT_DIR/../.." && pwd)
cd "$REPO_ROOT" || { echo "FATAL: cannot cd to repo root $REPO_ROOT"; exit 1; }

C_OK=""; C_BAD=""; C_OFF=""; C_DIM=""
if [ -t 1 ]; then
  C_OK=$'\033[32m'; C_BAD=$'\033[31m'; C_OFF=$'\033[0m'; C_DIM=$'\033[2m'
fi

# ------------------------------------------------- single source of truth ----
# Every assertion below reads these three and nothing else. Changing the
# product's name is one edit here plus the files it polices - never a hunt.
FULL_NAME='Ágora Transparency'
SHORT_NAME='Ágora'
PACKAGE='drupal/agora_transparency'

# The ERE that decides whether a file NAMES the product, for the closed world.
# The bare form without the accent is included deliberately: a packaged file
# that spells the name wrong is still a file naming the product, and the closed
# world should surface it rather than let it pass for something unrelated.
PRODUCT_ERE='Ágora|Agora|agora_transparency'

# Identity files: read by someone with no other context on the same surface.
# AGENTS.md and recommended.yml are load-bearing for a structural reason
# (D-021): AGENTS.md is scaffolded into the END USER's site root by
# composer.json's drupal-scaffold.file-mapping, and recommended.yml is consumed
# by Project Browser BY PERMALINK - both are read with no README adjacent.
#
# content/PEOPLE.md and content/MEDIA-LICENCES.md joined the list on 2026-08-26
# (T-905, T-904). They are IDENTITY files rather than prose-only, and the test
# is D-021's own: is this read by someone with no other context on the same
# surface? Both are shipped in the tarball, both make a claim a marketplace
# reviewer is entitled to check on its own terms - "every person here is
# invented", "every media file here is licensed" - and neither sits below a
# heading that has already named the product. The closed world caught them the
# moment they were written, with `findings: 2`, which is the mechanism working
# rather than an obstacle to route around.
IDENTITY_FILES='recipe.yml
composer.json
README.md
AGENTS.md
recommended.yml
content/PEOPLE.md
content/MEDIA-LICENCES.md'

# Prose-only: packaged files that legitimately mention the product below a
# surface that has already named it, or in a comment addressed to a maintainer
# rather than to an evaluator. Adding a file here is a DECLARATION, and it is
# printed on every run so it cannot be made quietly.
PROSE_ONLY='.gitattributes
.gitignore'

# The two quote characters as variables, so no pattern below needs a backslash.
SQ="'"
DQ='"'

FINDINGS=0
WORKDIR=$(mktemp -d -t identity-strings.XXXXXX) || { echo "FATAL: mktemp -d failed"; exit 1; }
trap 'rm -rf "$WORKDIR"' EXIT INT TERM

finding() { # <file> <line> <reason>
  FINDINGS=$((FINDINGS + 1))
  printf '  %s%s:%s%s  %s\n' "$C_BAD" "${1#./}" "$2" "$C_OFF" "$3"
}

note() { printf '  %s%s%s\n' "$C_DIM" "$1" "$C_OFF"; }

fatal() { # <reason>
  printf '\n%sFATAL:%s %s\n' "$C_BAD" "$C_OFF" "$1"
  printf 'This run has NO result, which is not a clean result (I-029).\n'
  exit 1
}

grep_fatal() { # <rc> <pattern> <subject>
  fatal "grep exited $1 on $3 while matching: $2 - a file that could not be searched is NOT a file that is correct (I-027)."
}

printf '=========================================================================================================\n'
printf 'identity-strings - Agora - D-021: identity strings carry the full name (T-322)\n'
printf 'repo: %s\n' "$REPO_ROOT"
printf '=========================================================================================================\n'
printf '\nsource of truth\n'
note "FULL_NAME:  $FULL_NAME"
note "SHORT_NAME: $SHORT_NAME"
note "PACKAGE:    $PACKAGE"

# ------------------------------------------------------------- preflight -----
command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1 || fatal 'jq is not available - assertions 2 and 3 read composer.json with it and cannot be evaluated.'
command -v git >/dev/null 2>&1 || fatal 'git is not available - the packaged scope cannot be resolved.'

IDENTITY_COUNT=0
MISSING_IDENTITY=''
while IFS= read -r f; do
  [ -n "$f" ] || continue
  IDENTITY_COUNT=$((IDENTITY_COUNT + 1))
  [ -r "$f" ] || MISSING_IDENTITY="$MISSING_IDENTITY $f"
done <<EOF
$IDENTITY_FILES
EOF

# T-321(b): defaulted because zero means FAIL here - the safe direction of
# I-031. Do not copy the `:-0` to a guard where zero means PASS.
if [ "${IDENTITY_COUNT:-0}" -eq 0 ]; then
  fatal 'zero identity files declared - the scope collapsed to nothing (I-007).'
fi
if [ -n "$MISSING_IDENTITY" ]; then
  fatal "declared identity file(s) not readable in the working tree:$MISSING_IDENTITY - the assertions about them cannot be evaluated, and absent is not correct."
fi

PROSE_COUNT=0
while IFS= read -r f; do
  [ -n "$f" ] || continue
  PROSE_COUNT=$((PROSE_COUNT + 1))
done <<EOF
$PROSE_ONLY
EOF

printf '\ndeclared scope\n'
note "identity files ($IDENTITY_COUNT): $(printf '%s' "$IDENTITY_FILES" | tr '\n' ' ')"
note "prose-only ($PROSE_COUNT): $(printf '%s' "$PROSE_ONLY" | tr '\n' ' ')"

# ----------------------------------------------------- YAML scalar reader ----
# recipe.yml is read with sed, never with a YAML parser: gate A deliberately
# owns no YAML dependency (T-320(c)). The reader therefore handles exactly the
# three forms a scalar can take on one line - bare, single-quoted,
# double-quoted - and REFUSES anything else loudly instead of guessing.
yaml_scalar() { # <key> <file>  -> prints the value
  _key=$1; _file=$2
  _matched=$(grep "^$_key:" "$_file" 2>/dev/null)
  _rc=$?
  [ "$_rc" -ge 2 ] && grep_fatal "$_rc" "^$_key:" "$_file"
  [ "$_rc" -eq 0 ] || return 1
  _v=$(printf '%s\n' "$_matched" | head -1 | sed "s/^$_key:[[:space:]]*//")
  case "$_v" in
    '|'* | '>'*)
      fatal "$_file: $_key: is a YAML block scalar, which this reader does not parse. Assertions 1 and 3 would compare a marker instead of a value; fix the reader or the file, never let it pass."
      ;;
  esac
  case "$_v" in
    "$SQ"*"$SQ")
      _v=${_v#"$SQ"}
      _v=${_v%"$SQ"}
      _v=$(printf '%s' "$_v" | sed "s/$SQ$SQ/$SQ/g")
      ;;
    "$DQ"*"$DQ")
      _v=${_v#"$DQ"}
      _v=${_v%"$DQ"}
      ;;
  esac
  printf '%s' "$_v"
}

# ------------------------------------------ 1. recipe.yml name: exactly one --
printf '\n1. recipe.yml name: - exactly one line, value is the full name\n'
NAME_MATCHED=$(grep '^name:' recipe.yml 2>/dev/null)
NAME_RC=$?
[ "$NAME_RC" -ge 2 ] && grep_fatal "$NAME_RC" '^name:' recipe.yml
if [ "$NAME_RC" -eq 0 ]; then
  NAME_LINES=$(printf '%s\n' "$NAME_MATCHED" | wc -l | tr -d ' ')
else
  NAME_LINES=0
fi
note "^name: lines: $NAME_LINES"
if [ "${NAME_LINES:-0}" -ne 1 ]; then
  finding recipe.yml '<name>' "expected exactly one ^name: line, found $NAME_LINES - the installer shows one of them and nobody can say which"
else
  RECIPE_NAME=$(yaml_scalar name recipe.yml)
  note "value: $RECIPE_NAME"
  if [ "$RECIPE_NAME" != "$FULL_NAME" ]; then
    finding recipe.yml '<name>' "is [$RECIPE_NAME], expected [$FULL_NAME] - D-021: this string IS the template selector's whole user interface"
  fi
fi

# ------------------------------------------------- 2. composer.json .name ----
printf '\n2. composer.json .name - the package identifier\n'
COMPOSER_NAME=$(jq -r '.name // empty' composer.json 2>/dev/null | tr -d '\r')
JQ_RC=$?
if [ "$JQ_RC" -ne 0 ]; then
  fatal 'composer.json could not be parsed by jq - assertions 2 and 3 have no value to compare.'
fi
note "value: $COMPOSER_NAME"
if [ "$COMPOSER_NAME" != "$PACKAGE" ]; then
  finding composer.json '<name>' "is [$COMPOSER_NAME], expected [$PACKAGE]"
fi

# ------------------------------------- 3. the two descriptions, byte-equal ---
printf '\n3. description - recipe.yml and composer.json byte-identical\n'
RECIPE_DESC=$(yaml_scalar description recipe.yml) || RECIPE_DESC=''
COMPOSER_DESC=$(jq -r '.description // empty' composer.json 2>/dev/null | tr -d '\r')
note "recipe.yml:    [$RECIPE_DESC]"
note "composer.json: [$COMPOSER_DESC]"
if [ -z "$RECIPE_DESC" ]; then
  finding recipe.yml '<description>' 'empty or absent - two surfaces cannot be kept in step when one of them says nothing'
elif [ -z "$COMPOSER_DESC" ]; then
  finding composer.json '<description>' 'empty or absent - two surfaces cannot be kept in step when one of them says nothing'
elif [ "$RECIPE_DESC" != "$COMPOSER_DESC" ]; then
  finding recipe.yml '<description>' 'differs from composer.json .description - D-021 keeps the two byte-identical, or the template describes two different products depending on which surface you read'
fi

# -------------------------------- 4. first mention names the product in full --
printf '\n4. first mention of %s in each identity file names it in full\n' "$SHORT_NAME"
WITH_MENTION=0
while IFS= read -r f; do
  [ -n "$f" ] || continue
  NORM=$(sed -e 's/^[[:space:]]*[>#]*[[:space:]]*//' "$f" 2>/dev/null | tr '\n\t' '  ' | tr -s ' ')
  case "$NORM" in
    *"$SHORT_NAME"*) ;;
    *) note "$f: no accented mention (covered by assertion 2 where it applies)"; continue ;;
  esac
  WITH_MENTION=$((WITH_MENTION + 1))
  AFTER=${NORM#*"$SHORT_NAME"}
  case "$AFTER" in
    ' Transparency'*)
      note "$f: first mention is the full name"
      ;;
    *)
      finding "$f" '<first mention>' "the first $SHORT_NAME is not followed by Transparency (next 40 characters: [$(printf '%s' "$AFTER" | cut -c1-40)]) - D-021: the first mention on a surface carries the full name"
      ;;
  esac
done <<EOF
$IDENTITY_FILES
EOF
note "identity files carrying an accented mention: $WITH_MENTION of $IDENTITY_COUNT"
# I-028 canary. If NO identity file matched, the accent match itself has broken
# - a locale, an encoding or a mangled checkout - and every assertion above
# stopped discriminating while still reporting zero findings. That degenerate
# run must not be reported as clean. Zero means FAIL here, so the `:-0` is the
# safe direction (I-031).
if [ "${WITH_MENTION:-0}" -eq 0 ]; then
  fatal "no identity file contains $SHORT_NAME at all - the accent match has broken and assertion 4 discriminated nothing (I-028)."
fi

# ------------------------------------------------- 5. the closed world -------
printf '\n5. closed world - every packaged file naming the product is declared\n'
git rev-parse --verify HEAD >/dev/null 2>&1 || fatal 'no git HEAD - the packaged scope cannot be resolved.'
ARCHIVE_LIST=$(git archive --format=tar HEAD 2>/dev/null | tar -tf - 2>/dev/null)
ARCHIVE_RC=$?
if [ "$ARCHIVE_RC" -ne 0 ] || [ -z "$ARCHIVE_LIST" ]; then
  fatal "git archive HEAD produced nothing (exit $ARCHIVE_RC) - an empty archive would pass every closed-world check through the back door."
fi
PACKAGED_MATCHED=$(printf '%s\n' "$ARCHIVE_LIST" | grep -v '/$' 2>/dev/null)
PKG_RC=$?
[ "$PKG_RC" -ge 2 ] && grep_fatal "$PKG_RC" 'directory entries' 'the archive listing'
printf '%s\n' "$PACKAGED_MATCHED" | grep -v '^$' > "$WORKDIR/scope" 2>/dev/null

# Early-warning half: untracked, non-ignored files that are not under an
# export-ignored root. Roots are read from .gitattributes; `git check-attr` is
# NOT usable here, verified 2026-08-23 - it reports nothing for a file inside a
# directory whose DIRECTORY entry carries the attribute, which is exactly how
# every root below is written.
IGNORED_ROOTS=''
if [ -r .gitattributes ]; then
  IGNORED_ROOTS=$(grep -E '^/[^[:space:]]+[[:space:]]+export-ignore' .gitattributes 2>/dev/null | sed 's|^/||' | sed 's|[[:space:]].*$||')
  IGN_RC=$?
  [ "$IGN_RC" -ge 2 ] && grep_fatal "$IGN_RC" 'export-ignore roots' .gitattributes
fi
UNTRACKED_ADDED=0
while IFS= read -r f; do
  [ -n "$f" ] || continue
  _skip=no
  while IFS= read -r r; do
    [ -n "$r" ] || continue
    case "$f" in "$r" | "$r"/*) _skip=yes ;; esac
  done <<EOF
$IGNORED_ROOTS
EOF
  [ "$_skip" = yes ] && continue
  printf '%s\n' "$f" >> "$WORKDIR/scope"
  UNTRACKED_ADDED=$((UNTRACKED_ADDED + 1))
done <<EOF
$(git ls-files --others --exclude-standard 2>/dev/null)
EOF

sort -u "$WORKDIR/scope" > "$WORKDIR/sorted" && mv "$WORKDIR/sorted" "$WORKDIR/scope"
SCOPE_COUNT=$(wc -l < "$WORKDIR/scope" | tr -d ' ')
note "packaged at HEAD plus $UNTRACKED_ADDED untracked candidate(s) = $SCOPE_COUNT file(s) in scope"
if [ "${SCOPE_COUNT:-0}" -eq 0 ]; then
  fatal 'the packaged scope listed 0 files - the result proves nothing (I-007).'
fi

NAMING=0
UNREADABLE=0
while IFS= read -r f; do
  [ -n "$f" ] || continue
  if [ -r "$f" ]; then
    _src=$f
  else
    _src="$WORKDIR/blob"
    git show "HEAD:$f" > "$_src" 2>/dev/null || { UNREADABLE=$((UNREADABLE + 1)); continue; }
  fi
  LC_ALL=C grep -qIE "$PRODUCT_ERE" "$_src" 2>/dev/null
  _rc=$?
  [ "$_rc" -ge 2 ] && grep_fatal "$_rc" "$PRODUCT_ERE" "$f"
  [ "$_rc" -eq 0 ] || continue
  NAMING=$((NAMING + 1))
  _declared=no
  while IFS= read -r d; do
    [ -n "$d" ] || continue
    [ "$f" = "$d" ] && _declared=identity
  done <<EOF
$IDENTITY_FILES
EOF
  if [ "$_declared" = no ]; then
    while IFS= read -r d; do
      [ -n "$d" ] || continue
      [ "$f" = "$d" ] && _declared=prose
    done <<EOF
$PROSE_ONLY
EOF
  fi
  case "$_declared" in
    identity) note "$f: names the product - declared IDENTITY" ;;
    prose)    note "$f: names the product - declared PROSE-ONLY" ;;
    *)        finding "$f" '<undeclared>' 'packaged file names the product but is declared neither as an identity file nor as prose-only - declare it in tests/bin/identity-strings, or stop naming the product in it (I-024)' ;;
  esac
done < "$WORKDIR/scope"

[ "$UNREADABLE" -gt 0 ] && note "$UNREADABLE packaged path(s) readable from neither the working tree nor HEAD"

# I-028 canary, the other direction: if NOTHING in the packaged tree names the
# product, the matcher has broken and the closed world enclosed nothing while
# still reporting zero findings. Zero means FAIL, so `:-0` is the safe
# direction here too (I-031).
if [ "${NAMING:-0}" -eq 0 ]; then
  fatal 'no packaged file names the product at all - the matcher has broken and the closed world enclosed nothing (I-028).'
fi

# A declared identity file that is not packaged is a real regression, but it is
# a finding rather than a FATAL: the scan itself still discriminated.
while IFS= read -r d; do
  [ -n "$d" ] || continue
  if ! grep -qxF "$d" "$WORKDIR/scope" 2>/dev/null; then
    finding "$d" '<not packaged>' 'declared as an identity file but absent from the packaged scope - either it stopped shipping, or the declaration has rotted'
  fi
done <<EOF
$IDENTITY_FILES
EOF

# ------------------------- 6. recipe.yml keeps its structure (D-032, D-011) --
# The hole this closes is argued in full in the header. In one line: a
# regenerated recipe.yml keeps `name:` and `description:` and loses everything
# else, so assertions 1 and 3 alone would wave it through.
printf '\n6. recipe.yml structure - the recipes: list and the area labels survived\n'

# The floor for `-- area:` lines. Deliberately BELOW today's count: see the
# header - this discriminates the loss of every comment in the file, which is
# what regeneration does, and it is not a per-label roll call.
AREA_MIN=15

RECIPES_ENTRIES='n/a'
AREA_LABELS='n/a'

if [ ! -r recipe.yml ]; then
  # UNREACHABLE IN THIS REPOSITORY: recipe.yml is a declared identity file and
  # the preflight has already exited FATAL if it is unreadable. The branch
  # exists for drupal/agora_theme's adapted copy (D-028), which has no
  # recipe.yml and must not be failed for not having one.
  note 'recipe.yml is not present - assertion 6 does not apply to this repository'
else
  RECIPES_KEY_MATCHED=$(grep '^recipes:' recipe.yml 2>/dev/null)
  RK_RC=$?
  [ "$RK_RC" -ge 2 ] && grep_fatal "$RK_RC" '^recipes:' recipe.yml
  if [ "$RK_RC" -eq 0 ]; then
    RECIPES_KEYS=$(printf '%s\n' "$RECIPES_KEY_MATCHED" | wc -l | tr -d ' ')
  else
    RECIPES_KEYS=0
  fi
  note "^recipes: keys: $RECIPES_KEYS"

  # The block runs from the key to the next line that starts a top-level key.
  # sed searches for the end pattern from the line AFTER the start, so the key
  # line cannot terminate its own range. Read with sed rather than a YAML
  # parser for the same reason as yaml_scalar above: gate A owns no YAML
  # dependency (T-320(c)).
  RECIPES_ENTRIES=0
  if [ "${RECIPES_KEYS:-0}" -ge 1 ]; then
    ENTRIES_MATCHED=$(sed -n '/^recipes:/,/^[A-Za-z_]/p' recipe.yml 2>/dev/null | grep '^  - ' 2>/dev/null)
    EN_RC=$?
    [ "$EN_RC" -ge 2 ] && grep_fatal "$EN_RC" '^  - ' 'the recipes: block of recipe.yml'
    if [ "$EN_RC" -eq 0 ]; then
      RECIPES_ENTRIES=$(printf '%s\n' "$ENTRIES_MATCHED" | wc -l | tr -d ' ')
    fi
  fi
  note "entries beneath it: $RECIPES_ENTRIES (census, NOT a threshold - units 004 and 005 add more)"

  # Zero means FAIL for both guards below, which is the only direction in which
  # the `:-0` default is safe (I-031). The two are chained with `elif` on
  # purpose: a missing key is ONE defect, and reporting it a second time as an
  # empty list would inflate the finding count without adding information.
  if [ "${RECIPES_KEYS:-0}" -eq 0 ]; then
    finding recipe.yml '<recipes>' 'no top-level recipes: key - the file has lost the list of recipes applied before this one, which is what a regenerated recipe.yml looks like (D-032). Restore it from git, do not retype it'
  elif [ "${RECIPES_ENTRIES:-0}" -eq 0 ]; then
    finding recipe.yml '<recipes>' 'the recipes: key lists no entries - an empty list applies nothing, and installs a site template that is not this site template'
  fi

  AREA_MATCHED=$(grep -e '-- area:' recipe.yml 2>/dev/null)
  AR_RC=$?
  [ "$AR_RC" -ge 2 ] && grep_fatal "$AR_RC" '-- area:' recipe.yml
  if [ "$AR_RC" -eq 0 ]; then
    AREA_LABELS=$(printf '%s\n' "$AREA_MATCHED" | wc -l | tr -d ' ')
  else
    AREA_LABELS=0
  fi
  note "lines matching -- area: $AREA_LABELS (floor $AREA_MIN)"
  if [ "${AREA_LABELS:-0}" -lt "$AREA_MIN" ]; then
    finding recipe.yml '<area labels>' "only $AREA_LABELS line(s) match -- area:, expected at least $AREA_MIN - the functional-area seams of D-011 rider b are comments, and comments are exactly what an export drops on the floor"
  fi
fi

# ----------------------------------------------------------------- summary ---
printf '\n=========================================================================================================\n'
printf 'identity files checked: %s · prose-only declared: %s · packaged files naming the product: %s · recipes entries: %s · area label lines: %s · findings: %s\n' \
  "$IDENTITY_COUNT" "$PROSE_COUNT" "$NAMING" "$RECIPES_ENTRIES" "$AREA_LABELS" "$FINDINGS"
printf '=========================================================================================================\n'

if [ "$FINDINGS" -eq 0 ]; then
  # The colour token is passed as its own conversion, never glued to the word
  # that follows it: a format specifier followed immediately by lowercase
  # letters reads to a spell checker as ONE token, which is how `snone` and
  # `smention` ended up as entries in .cspell-project-words.txt. Keeping the
  # literal in its own argument costs nothing and adds no dictionary entry.
  printf '%s%s%s\n' "$C_OK" 'identity-strings: clean' "$C_OFF"
  exit 0
fi
printf '%s%s%s%s%s\n' "$C_BAD" 'identity-strings: ' "$FINDINGS" ' finding(s)' "$C_OFF"
exit 1
